\title{Multi-Head Attention}
\description{
Multi-head attention as described in \emph{Attention Is All You Need}.

This is a thin wrapper around \code{\link[torch:nn_multihead_attention]{torch::nn_multihead_attention()}} that makes it usable as a
building block of a \code{\link[mlr3pipelines:Graph]{Graph}} of tensor operations, where both
self-attention and cross-attention can be expressed, see section \emph{Input and Output Channels}.
}
\section{Tensor Layout}{

All inputs and outputs are \verb{(batch, sequence, feature)}, i.e. the \code{batch_first} layout of
\code{\link[torch:nn_multihead_attention]{torch::nn_multihead_attention()}}.
Unlike in \code{torch}, where \code{batch_first} defaults to \code{FALSE} and inputs are
\verb{(sequence, batch, feature)}, this is fixed and cannot be changed: like every other operator,
this one is part of a network whose shapes all have the batch size in their first dimension.
Operators read dimensions by position -- a convolution reads dimension 2 as the channel
dimension, and the shape inference reports dimension 1 as the batch size -- so a tensor whose
first dimension is the sequence would be misinterpreted downstream.

The attention weights (see the construction argument \code{need_weights}) are batch-first in \code{torch}
as well, so their shape is unaffected.
}

\section{nn_module}{

Calls \code{\link[torch:nn_multihead_attention]{torch::nn_multihead_attention()}} when trained, where the parameters \code{embed_dim}, \code{kdim}
and \code{vdim} are inferred as the last dimension of the query, key and value tensors respectively,
and \code{batch_first} is always \code{TRUE}, see section \emph{Tensor Layout}.
}

\section{Parameters}{

\itemize{
\item \code{num_heads} :: \code{integer(1)}\cr
Number of parallel attention heads. The embedding dimension must be divisible by \code{num_heads}.
\item \code{dropout} :: \code{numeric(1)}\cr
Dropout probability on the attention weights.
Default is \code{0}.
\item \code{bias} :: \code{logical(1)}\cr
Whether to add a bias to the input and output projections.
Default is \code{TRUE}.
\item \code{add_bias_kv} :: \code{logical(1)}\cr
Whether to add a bias to the key and value sequences at dimension 1.
Default is \code{FALSE}.
\item \code{add_zero_attn} :: \code{logical(1)}\cr
Whether to add a new batch of zeros to the key and value sequences at dimension 1.
Default is \code{FALSE}.
\item \code{avg_weights} :: \code{logical(1)}\cr
Whether the returned attention weights are averaged over the attention heads.
Default is \code{TRUE}. Only has an effect when the construction argument \code{need_weights} is \code{TRUE}.
}

Note that \code{embed_dim}, \code{kdim} and \code{vdim} are \emph{not} parameters, as they are inferred from the
shapes of the input tensors, and that \code{batch_first} is \emph{not} a parameter either, as it is fixed
to \code{TRUE}, see section \emph{Tensor Layout}.
}

\section{Input and Output Channels}{

The number of input channels is determined by the construction argument \code{mode}:
\itemize{
\item \code{mode = "self"} (default): one input channel \code{"input"}, which is used as query, key and value,
i.e. the \code{PipeOp} performs \emph{self-attention}.
\item \code{mode = "cross"}: input channels \code{"query"} and \code{"key_value"}, i.e. the \code{PipeOp} performs
\emph{cross-attention}, where the second input is used as both key and value.
\item \code{mode = "general"}: input channels \code{"query"}, \code{"key"} and \code{"value"}, i.e. the \code{PipeOp} performs
\emph{cross-attention} with separate key and value inputs.
}

The number of output channels is determined by the construction argument \code{need_weights}:
\itemize{
\item \code{need_weights = FALSE} (default): one output channel \code{"output"}, containing the attention
output.
\item \code{need_weights = TRUE}: output channels \code{"output"} and \code{"weights"}, where the latter contains
the attention weights.
}

For an explanation see \code{\link{PipeOpTorch}}.
}

